  "summary": "Airbrushed sound-system posters with maximum contrast, minimum rules.",
  "description": "Hand-painted dancehall posters are Jamaica's loudest vernacular design: billboard-scale boards and banners announcing sessions of Black Scorpio, Stone Love or King Tubby's sound systems, painted by signmen like Wilfred Limonious, Basil \"Witty\" Williams and Usain Bolt's uncle Jerry in furious fluorescent color. Crash-lettering, torn-edge banners, cartoon figures and ragged raster dots made every poster a one-shot composition read at passing-bus speed. Art historian Faith Smith's \"Stylin'\" scholarship and the One Love Books volume \"Dancehall\" canonized the style's influence on graphic designers worldwide before vinyl banners erased the hand-painted era.",
  "culturalContext": "Jamaica's hand-painted dancehall posters turned sound-system commerce into vernacular design genius; signmen like Limonious made every corner a gallery, the style influenced international graphic designers through \"Dancehall\" book scholarship, and its crash-lettering grammar survives in Jamaican music branding and global street design.",
